f 1038 #edb/ref 17592186380406 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133870 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186430807 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133870 #edb/ref 1039 "Determining Whether a Set of Polynomials is Orthogonal With Respect to a Given Inner Product" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133870 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186133686 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133870 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "c2bcfcb6-8f8b-5aa9-8346-05db880fa5bb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133871 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049811 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133871 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050331 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133871 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134560 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133871 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134568 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133871 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362380 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133871 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362382 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133871 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362395 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133871 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362396 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133871 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186430805 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133871 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186430806 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133871 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186430808 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133871 #edb/ref 1039 "Determining Whether a Set of Vectors Is Orthogonal With Respect to a Given Inner Product" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133871 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186133687 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133871 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "67683e7d-bf4f-5fdf-8fe5-df996c30e836" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133872 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050929 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133872 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134561 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133872 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134569 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133872 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272112 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133872 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272132 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133872 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362390 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133872 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362391 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133872 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362392 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133872 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362393 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133872 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362394 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133872 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362397 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133872 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186382668 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133872 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186382675 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133872 #edb/ref 1039 "Determining Whether a Set of Functions is Orthogonal With Respect to a Given Inner Product" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133872 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186133688 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133872 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "382a6db7-2708-5711-885e-6f3f3037e8df" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133991 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134813 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133991 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134814 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133991 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134815 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133991 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134816 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133991 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133991 #edb/ref 1045 "Orthogonal Sets in Inner Product Spaces" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133991 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186134813 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133991 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048572 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133991 #edb/ref 1050 780.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133991 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "77b214ca-9157-567d-b810-012cd4c3adca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134569 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186133452 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134569 #edb/ref 1069 "q-99808" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134569 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following set of functions:\n$f_{1}(t) = t^{2}, f_{2}(t) = t^{3}, f_{3}(t) = t$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[- 1, 1]$ of continuous functions over $[- 1, 1]$ is defined as\n$⟨f(t), g(t)⟩ = \\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{1}f(t)g(t)dt$,\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $f_{1}(t)⊥f_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n2. $f_{2}(t)⊥f_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n3. $S = {f_{1}(t), f_{2}(t), f_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product\n\n*Hint: $⟨f_{1}, f_{3}⟩ = 0$.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134569 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a07c5a93-5161-56bc-aa10-7d11733928e4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134813 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134815 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134813 #edb/ref 1061 32389 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134813 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "520866ac-288a-5e25-aa54-cc9335b03e13" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134813 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135104 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134814 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134816 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134814 #edb/ref 1061 32390 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134814 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "01441c28-8b4e-5675-bb73-a2623682a7a8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134814 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133870 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134815 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134814 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134815 #edb/ref 1061 32391 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134815 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "45404b46-33b9-5949-bfc4-bc3b2e9e0898" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134815 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133871 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134816 #edb/ref 1061 32392 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134816 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9c764926-de30-5778-b9bb-86fbaf7b9c0e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134816 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133872 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135104 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's consider an inner product space $V$ equipped with the inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle.$ \nA set of vectors \n\n$$\n{U}=\\{ \\mathbf{u}_1, \\cdots, \\mathbf{u}_p \\}\n$$\n\nis called an **orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product** if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal.\n\nFor example, let's consider the set\n\n![](images/69/69571997d87ed3f40ee7e9b0742075fbd5ee107892f6a9b4198f75f3f5a9fce3.svg)\n\nand the inner product on $\\mathbb{R}^3$ defined as \n\n$$\n\\langle \\mathbf{x},\\mathbf{y} \\rangle = x_1 y_1 + 3x_2 y_2 + 2x_3 y_3.\n$$\n\nIf we denote the vectors in our set ${V}$ as $\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3 \\},$ we can check that ${V}$ is orthogonal by showing that \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{2}⟩=0 \\\\ ⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{3}⟩=0 \\\\ ⟨{v}_{2},{v}_{3}⟩=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's compute each inner product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{2}⟩ & =(1)(2)+3(1)(2)+2(1)(-4)=2+6-8=0 & ✓ \\\\ ⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{3}⟩ & =(1)(-3)+3(1)(1)+2(1)(0)=-3+3+0=0 & ✓ \\\\ ⟨{v}_{2},{v}_{3}⟩ & =(2)(-3)+3(2)(1)+2(-4)(0)=-6+6+0=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo indeed, ${V}$ is an orthogonal set." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135104 #edb/ref 1065 5149 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135104 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f5f69210-8732-5a94-b16b-b7de520cfc66" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135104 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237781 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186462330 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186237781 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186462331 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186237781 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186462332 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186237781 #edb/ref 1069 "q-100114" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237781 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237781 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following set of polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=2t+1,\\,{p}_{2}(t)=3t,\\,{p}_{3}(t)=t+1.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space ${R}_{1}[t]$ of polynomials of degree at most $1$ in the variable $t$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(0)q(0)+p(-1)q(-1),\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** ${p}_{1}(t)⊥̸{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- **II.** ${p}_{2}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- **III.** $S={{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186237781 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ea1a91d4-93f7-5e8d-b2ce-c7694a823cec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186237781 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨2t+1,3t⟩ \\\\  & =(2t+1){|}_{0}\\cdot (3t){|}_{0}+(2t+1){|}_{-1}\\cdot (3t){|}_{-1} \\\\  & =(2(0)+1)\\cdot (3(0))+(2(-1)+1)\\cdot (3(-1)) \\\\  & =3\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥̸{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨3t,t+1⟩ \\\\  & =(3t){|}_{0}\\cdot (t+1){|}_{0}+(3t){|}_{-1}\\cdot (t+1){|}_{-1} \\\\  & =(3(0))\\cdot (0+1)+(3(-1))\\cdot ((-1)+1) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{2}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${p}_{1}(t)⊥̸{p}_{2}(t),$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362382 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186470604 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186362382 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186470605 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186362382 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186470606 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186362382 #edb/ref 1069 "q-99656" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362382 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362382 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors shown above. Given that the inner product in the vector space ${R}^{3}$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+2{x}_{3}{y}_{3},\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{2}$ with respect to the given inner product\n- **II.** ${v}_{2}⊥̸{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product\n- **III.** $S={{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186362382 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "689e4d4d-2277-5c62-9cd6-9887b81429d8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362382 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{2}⟩ & =(1)(4)+2(1)(-1)+2(-1)(1) \\\\  & =4-2-2 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{2}$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is false since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{2},{v}_{3}⟩ & =(4)(0)+2(-1)(1)+2(1)(1) \\\\  & =0-2+2 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{2}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, computing the inner product $⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{3}⟩$, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{3}⟩ & =(1)(0)+2(1)(1)+2(-1)(1) \\\\  & =0+2-2 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product.\nAs a result, the set $S$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362397 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186470628 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186362397 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186470629 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186362397 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186470630 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186362397 #edb/ref 1069 "q-99951" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362397 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362397 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)={e}^{t},\\,{f}_{2}(t)=t{e}^{-t}-{e}^{-t},\\,{f}_{3}(t)={e}^{-t}\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[0,2]$ of continuous functions over $[0,2]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- **II.** ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- **III.** $S={{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186362397 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d313e454-e9a2-5532-b1b1-37c4d7cbbe80" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362397 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is called orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨{e}^{t},t{e}^{-t}-{e}^{-t}⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{t}(t{e}^{-t}-{e}^{-t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(t-1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}-t){|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}-2-(0-0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨{e}^{t},{e}^{-t}⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =t{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2-0 \\\\  & =2\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{3}(t),$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382675 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186472413 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186382675 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186472414 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186382675 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186472415 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186382675 #edb/ref 1069 "q-99949" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382675 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382675 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,{f}_{2}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{f}_{3}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[-\\pi /2,\\pi /2]$ of continuous functions over $[-\\pi /2,\\pi /2]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- **II.** ${f}_{2}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- **III.** $S={{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186382675 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "866dfd7d-0156-5915-b7a2-48bb304694df" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382675 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is called orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the identity $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ))$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)){|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ differentiating we have $\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$ Hence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}{sin}^{2}⁡t{|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-{sin}^{2}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1-1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{2}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{2}(t),$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186408166 #edb/ref 10 :answer-field.type/checkbox #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186428606 #edb/ref 1030 0.5 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428606 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account linear: 1 rises / 2 eligible passed source activities = 0.500000; nearest selected fraction = 1/2. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: linear=1/2 (1/2). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: True. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/linear-frequency-estimates.json, pair 2104->2094. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428606 #edb/ref 1036 #edb/ref 17592186049045 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428607 #edb/ref 1030 0.5 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428607 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account linear: 1 rises / 2 eligible passed source activities = 0.500000; nearest selected fraction = 1/2. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: linear=1/2 (1/2). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: True. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-ban